The three parts, verified:

```js
// 1. BASE CASE — the stopping condition; must be reachable
// 2. RECURSIVE CASE — a STRICTLY SMALLER input
// 3. COMBINE — how sub-answers form the answer

function factorial(n) {
  if (n <= 1) return 1;              // base
  return n * factorial(n - 1);       // smaller + combine
}

// THE EXPONENTIAL TRAP — naive fib: O(2^n), same subtrees recomputed
function fibSlow(n) {
  if (n <= 1) return n;
  return fibSlow(n - 1) + fibSlow(n - 2);  // fib(30) = ~1M calls
}

// MEMOIZED — O(n): the map remembers every computed answer
function fibFast(n, memo = new Map()) {
  if (n <= 1) return n;
  if (memo.has(n)) return memo.get(n);
  const val = fibFast(n - 1, memo) + fibFast(n - 2, memo);
  memo.set(n, val);                   // cache BEFORE returning
  return val;
}
```

The depth discipline (the JS-specific part): every recursive call adds a frame; the engine's stack is finite (~10k frames typical, engine-dependent). Linear recursion — recursing once per element of an n-list — overflows at real input sizes; recursion DEPTH should be O(log n) (balanced divide-and-conquer: binary recursion on halves is depth log n — mergesort, BST walks) or bounded by structure height (trees are usually safe; flat lists are not). When a recursion overflows, convert to iteration with an explicit stack (an array you push/pop) — the SAME algorithm, heap instead of call stack.

The interview frame: recursion is the natural language of trees and nested structures (JSON traversal, DOM walks, directory scans) — forcing iteration there is harder to read. For sequences, prefer iteration unless the recurrence is genuinely self-similar. Say aloud: 'depth is the cost driver; this recursion is O(log n) deep, so the stack is safe.'
